// The declarations the `declared` fixture's sheet leaves its type cells empty for.
//
// The point of the pair is that `declared-expanded` writes the same table with every
// member's type in its own cell, and the two produce the same file. So nothing here may
// say anything about a value that the other workbook does not also say.
//
// The bracketed keys are the exception, and deliberately so: a bound and a role are
// checked and acted on, and neither reaches the wire. The pair passing with them written
// on one side only is what says that.

/// One thing a row hands out.
struct Reward
    /// Which item, as that table's key.
    field itemId int
    /// How many of it.
    field count int (min=1, max=9999)
    /// Shown beside the count. Blank where there is nothing to show.
    field icon string? (asset=icon)

/// One line of a table whose rows below make up the record.
///
/// **Here so the notation reaches a multi-row group as well as a serial one.** A group whose
/// elements are the rows below it has one header column per member, and every element's field
/// carries that same cell - so the check asking "which column named the struct" counted the
/// elements and reported a group naming its struct twice, then reported every member as a
/// column no group claimed. Both sentences at once, and neither true.
/// spec/primary-layout.md section 4.3.
struct Bonus
    /// Which stat it raises.
    field statId int
    /// By how much.
    field amount int

/// What a reward is made of.
///
/// Declared here rather than in a sheet, so the type cell that names it is reading a
/// declaration this run read from a file.
enum Element
    /// Burns.
    value Fire = 1
    /// Freezes.
    value Ice = 2
